<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<!--
CycloneDDS config for running the robot side and the policy server over WiFi (experiment W, and
live-WiFi runs later). One file for both machines; each sets its own address and its peer's:

    -e EVH_SELF_IP=192.168.0.73 -e EVH_PEER_IP=192.168.0.50 \
    -e CYCLONEDDS_URI=file:///ws/docker/cyclonedds-wifi.xml

It keeps all three fixes the direct-cable configs needed (see cyclonedds-jetson.xml for the long
story of each), and each matters more on WiFi, not less:

  * the interface is pinned by address: both machines also have docker0 and tailscale0, and
    CycloneDDS advertising the wrong one fails silently;
  * discovery is unicast to the one known peer: multicast over WiFi is unreliable by design
    (sent at the lowest basic rate, often filtered by access points), and the first WiFi attempt
    never completed discovery at all;
  * FragmentSize stays below the MTU so large messages are fragmented by DDS, not by IP. Losing
    one IP fragment loses the whole datagram; on a lossy radio that turns every 21 KB camera frame
    into ~16 chances to lose it.

Leave the addresses static for a session (a DHCP lease change mid-run is indistinguishable from an
outage).
-->
<CycloneDDS xmlns="https://cdds.io/config">
  <Domain>
    <General>
      <Interfaces>
        <NetworkInterface address="${EVH_SELF_IP}"/>
      </Interfaces>
      <AllowMulticast>false</AllowMulticast>
      <FragmentSize>1344B</FragmentSize>
    </General>
    <Discovery>
      <ParticipantIndex>auto</ParticipantIndex>
      <MaxAutoParticipantIndex>20</MaxAutoParticipantIndex>
      <Peers>
        <Peer address="${EVH_SELF_IP}"/>
        <Peer address="${EVH_PEER_IP}"/>
      </Peers>
    </Discovery>
  </Domain>
</CycloneDDS>
